<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zeugitana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zeugitana was the northern region of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. The name was used for the old core of the province of Africa Proconsularis after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms around 303 separated Byzacena and Tripolitania. Fertile and densely settled, it was a major source of grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zeugitana.jpg" alt="Zeugitana" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Africa_Proconsularis_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
